\title{snpscaper: explore human genetic variation with 1000 Genomes data}
\description{
snpscaper is a teaching package. It lets you pick any region of the human
genome, fetch the genotypes of 2,504 people from 26 populations sampled by
the 1000 Genomes Project, and explore how genetic variation is distributed
within and between those populations. Everything happens inside R: there
is no need for the command line or for downloading files by hand.
\subsection{Key ideas, briefly}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{SNP} (single nucleotide polymorphism): a position in the genome where
people differ by a single DNA letter, for example some carry \code{G} and
others \code{A}. Each person carries two copies of every autosome, so at a SNP
they carry 0, 1 or 2 copies of the alternative letter (allele). snpscaper
stores genotypes as these counts.
\item \strong{VCF} (variant call format): the standard text file format for storing
genotypes. The 1000 Genomes Project publishes one compressed VCF per
chromosome, each tens of gigabytes. snpscaper reads only the small slice
you ask for, using an index file that tells it where in the big file a
region lives.
\item \strong{1000 Genomes Project}: an international project that sequenced the
genomes of people from 26 populations, grouped into five continental
"super-populations": African (AFR), Admixed American (AMR), East Asian
(EAS), European (EUR) and South Asian (SAS). See \link{samples_1kg}.
}
}

\subsection{Typical workflow}{
\enumerate{
\item \code{\link[=fetch_region]{fetch_region()}} gets genotypes for a gene or a set of coordinates.
\item \code{\link[=filter_snps]{filter_snps()}} removes rare and poorly genotyped SNPs, and optionally
SNPs that are strongly correlated with their neighbours (LD pruning).
\item \code{\link[=keep_pops]{keep_pops()}} narrows the data to the populations you want to compare.
\item \code{\link[=as_pca_table]{as_pca_table()}} prepares the data for principal component analysis
with the FactoMineR and factoextra packages.
}
}
